// Callers of `BundleFault`, and the fix the first version of this file asked for.
//
// **Written twice on 2026-09-05.** The first version had two callers — `forUser` and `forSelfCheck`
// — testing the one falsifiable claim the taxonomy in `../../../QUESTIONS.md` makes:
//
// > **provenance** — did this program produce the thing that failed? — the union can **never** carry
// > it, because it is a fact about the call site.
//
// A bundler is the sharpest provenance case there is, because half its input is its own output: a
// generated barrel, a synthesised entry and a rewritten specifier are all things the bundler wrote,
// and a `NoSuchModule` in one of them is our bug where the same fault in a user's file is theirs.
//
// The claim held and was wrong about *why*, so the second version applies the fix. §1 is what the fix
// cost and §2 is the thing it got wrong on the first attempt, which is the finding.

import { Result, Vec } from "core";
import { Out } from "std";
import {
  BundleFault, NoSuchModule, Cycle, TopLevelAwait, NoPrefix, Source, Origin
} from "@/packages/ts";

/** Who should read this, which is the only question either caller is asking. */
export enum Blame { User, Bundler }

/**
 * Who caused it — **one function now, where there were two constants.**
 *
 * The first version of this file had `forUser` and `forSelfCheck`, both constant on three of four
 * members, and argued that a constant classifier is the tell for a question the union cannot answer.
 * `@/packages/ts/src/bundle.wac` now records the answer where it is known — every path in a fault is
 * a `Source`, which carries an `Origin` the loader wrote — and the two collapse into this.
 *
 * It is a real classifier: three of the four arms read the payload and answer differently depending
 * on it.
 */
export Blame blameFor(BundleFault f) {
  match (f) {
    // `bundle` could not find a prefix no module already uses, after `tried` attempts. No user input
    // causes it and no user can act on it — the one member that needed no new data.
    NoPrefix { tried }:            { return Bundler; }
    // The importer decides it. A generated barrel importing something absent is our bug.
    NoSuchModule { spec, from }:   { return from.origin is Generated ? Bundler : User; }
    TopLevelAwait { at, span }:    { return at.origin is Generated ? Bundler : User; }
    // **A cycle is not one path, which is the member that made `Source` the right shape.** A flag on
    // the fault would have had to say *which* module it was about; here every module in the cycle
    // carries its own, and one generated module in the ring is enough to make the ring ours.
    Cycle { path }:                { return anyGenerated(path) ? Bundler : User; }
  }
}

/** Whether any module in a cycle is one this run produced. */
bool anyGenerated(const Vec<Source> path) {
  for (Source s in path.items()) { if (s.origin is Generated) { return true; } }
  return false;
}

/** `bundle: cannot resolve 'x' from 'y'`, or the internal-error form. */
export string say(const Blame b, BundleFault f) { … }

// ## 1. Two constants became one classifier, and the change was in the producer
//
// The first version's two functions were constant on three of four members. **A constant classifier
// is the tell** for a caller asking a question the union cannot answer: neither was classifying, each
// was asserting a blanket answer. `@/packages/box/src/cp.wac`'s `policy(Side, FileFault)` at least
// used the member.
//
// `blameFor` above reads the payload in three of four arms and answers differently depending on it,
// which is what a classifier is. Nothing about the caller changed to make that possible.
// `@/packages/ts/src/bundle.wac` changed: every path in a fault is now a `Source`, carrying an
// `Origin` the loader wrote.
//
// > **Provenance cannot be recovered by a caller and can be recorded by a producer.** Every earlier
// > instance had a caller who happened to know — `cp` knew which argument it passed, the renderer
// > knew it built the page — so the axis looked like caller knowledge. It is not. It belongs to
// > whoever raised the fault, and the four earlier cases were the ones where the caller had it too.
//
// The cost is a type change that propagates: `Module.path` is `Module.at` and every consumer of a
// `Module` sees a `Source` where it saw a `string`. That is the right cost — a path whose origin is
// unrecorded is the thing that was broken — and it is not a small one.
//
// ## 2. `bool fromGenerated` was the first shape and it is wrong, which is the finding
//
// The previous version of this file proposed `NoSuchModule { spec, from, fromGenerated: bool }` — a
// flag on the fault. Writing it out, it does not fit, and the member that breaks it is `Cycle`:
//
//     export struct Cycle { Vec<Source> path; }
//
// **A cycle is not one path.** A flag on the fault would have to say *which* module it was about, and
// there is no such module — the ring is the fault. So the flag would be `anyGenerated`, computed at
// the point the fault is raised, and a caller wanting *which one* could not ask.
//
// Which says where provenance actually lives:
//
// > **Provenance attaches to the thing that has an origin, not to the fault that mentions it.** The
// > four members mention one path, one path, a list of paths, and none; a fault-level flag has to
// > pick one of those four shapes and be wrong for the other three.
//
// Making `Source` a type puts it on the path, and every member then carries as many origins as it has
// paths, automatically. `NoPrefix` carries none, which is right — it is the one member with no path
// and the one that needed no new data.
//
// This is the same shape as `@/packages/wacc/src/decl.wac`'s `Program { Decl[] decls; Tok path; }`
// entry, from the other end: there a `Tok` was meaningless without knowing its file, here a path is
// meaningless without knowing who wrote it. **Both are a scalar that was always half of a pair**, and
// in both the fix is a type rather than a field.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **`say` takes a `Blame` and a fault and cannot check they belong together.** `say(User,
// NoPrefix(3))` type-checks and prints a user diagnostic for an internal error. The pair is
// `blameFor` applied to that fault and nothing says so — *paired values with nothing holding the
// pair*, fourth site, after `@/packages/git/example/ignorelint.wac`'s index-into-a-`Vec`.
//
// **`Origin` has two arms and wants three.** A module the *user* generated — a build step's output
// that this bundler merely read — is neither `UserWrote` nor `Generated` by this run, and it is the
// case where blaming either party is wrong. Two arms is what the bundler can distinguish, which is
// the rule `../../../QUESTIONS.md` records as *a fault union is bounded above by what its sources can
// distinguish*, arriving on an enum rather than a union.
//
// **A `Blame` is not in the return type of anything.** `bundle` answers `Result<Bytes, BundleFault>`
// and this classification happens after the value is gone. A bundler that wanted to *recover* from
// its own fault — retry with a different prefix, which is exactly what `NoPrefix` invites — cannot,
// because by the time it knows the fault is its own it has returned. Fourth instance of an `Err` that
// should carry what was recovered, after `@/packages/datetime`'s `Take.Accept`,
// `@/packages/box/src/cat.wac`'s halfway read and `@/packages/codec`'s `BadPadding`.
